# 7. Theory

## 7.1 Scope of the theoretical discussion

The empirical results of Sections 3–6 describe a measured phase decomposition of the lag-one covariance of locally detrended prime gaps. They do not by themselves identify a mechanism. This section states what a Hardy–Littlewood description of the phenomenon would look like, and states clearly what we do not claim.

## 7.2 Sieve-phase dependence

The observed dependence of the detrended gap on the opening-prime residue modulo a primorial is consistent with the general arithmetic principle that local congruence constraints modify the density of prime tuples. In a Hardy–Littlewood description, these local effects are encoded by singular-series factors. For consecutive gaps \(g = h\), one must additionally exclude configurations containing intermediate primes; this is precisely the role of an inclusion–exclusion correction on top of the pair-density heuristic.

A finite inclusion–exclusion calculation of this kind can provide a heuristic estimate of the consecutive-gap distribution. We do **not** use such a calculation to define our estimators or to claim an asymptotic prediction. It is mentioned here only to record that a natural theoretical counterpart of the empirical phase decomposition exists, and that it has not been used in the present paper.

## 7.3 The measured offset \(d\)

The quantity
\[
d = L - \operatorname{sd}(g)
\]
is reported as a **finite-range measurement** over the window \([10^{10}, 3\times 10^{10})\). Its observed value \(d = 2.631\) lies in the pre-registered band \([2.5, 2.7]\) for this window (Section 5), consistent with the value observed at \(10^9\) in Berramdane (2026a).

The observed level of \(d\), and its drift across logarithmic windows, should not be identified with a theorem-level constant. The present data establish the **reproducibility** of the phase-conditioned covariance decomposition; the derivation of the absolute value and the drift of \(d\) from first principles remains an open question.

## 7.4 Relation to existing heuristics

The relation to existing prime-in-interval variance heuristics is **qualitative**. Those frameworks concern cumulative counts or interval variances, whereas the present observable is the lag-one covariance of consecutive numerical gaps after local detrending. Any transfer of results between the two settings would require additional arguments, which are not supplied here.

Similarly, the Montgomery–Soundararajan framework provides a conditional variance-consistency benchmark for a cumulative correlation statistic. Applying that framework to a fixed number of consecutive gaps is an extrapolation beyond its stated asymptotic regime and is not made in this paper.

## 7.5 What is not claimed

We do not claim:

- that the phase decomposition explains all of the observed dependence;
- that the residual correlation \(\rho_{\text{resid}}\) converges to zero as \(M\) grows;
- that a Hardy–Littlewood or Montgomery–Soundararajan calculation proves the observed value of \(d\);
- that the present work constitutes a new theory of prime gaps;
- that the results extend beyond the tested finite range.

We claim only that the phase-conditioned covariance decomposition is exact and reproducible at \(10^{10}\), that the residual lag-one correlation remains negative at all seven tested moduli, and that the measured offset \(d\) is stable across the pre-registered ranges.

## 7.6 A finite inclusion–exclusion calculation as future work

A finite truncated inclusion–exclusion calculation for intermediate-prime exclusion is a possible diagnostic for the magnitude of \(d\). It is not required for the empirical claims of this paper and is not presented here as a prediction or a test. If it is performed in future work, it should be pre-registered with explicit choices of pair-density normalization, intermediate-offset truncation depth, tail treatment, and renormalization conventions, and reported as a **finite truncated inclusion–exclusion diagnostic** rather than as a hypothesis test.
